"""Report the measured reduction, independent uncertainty and guarded workflow."""
import json,hashlib,html,shutil,sys
from pathlib import Path
from analyze_dielectric3d import read

def pc(x):return 'undefined (null)' if x is None else f'{100*x:.4f}%'
def table(headers,rows):return '<table><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+html.escape(str(v))+'</th>' for v in headers)+'</tr>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+html.escape(str(v))+'</td>' for v in row)+'</tr>' for row in rows)+'</table>'

def main(root):
    work=root/'work/d3shadow';out=root/'outputs/differt-bridge/examples/dielectric3d_refinement';out.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True);a=read(work/'analysis.json');assert a['status']=='passed'
    p=next(r for r in a['summary'] if r['campaign']=='primary' and r['engine']=='sionna' and r['material']=='all');h=next(r for r in a['summary'] if r['campaign']=='holdout' and r['engine']=='sionna' and r['material']=='all')
    summary=table(['Population','Engine workflow','Material','Ray shadow RMS','Optional full-wave shadow RMS','Optional total RMS','Shadow channels'],[(r['campaign'],r['engine'],r['material'],pc(r['shadow_ray']['complex_rms']),pc(r['shadow_refined']['complex_rms']),pc(r['refined']['complex_rms']),r['shadow_channels']) for r in a['summary']])
    reference=table(['Population','Material','Fine/coarse reference','Total change','Shadow change'],[(r['campaign'],r['material'],Path(r['fine']).parent.name+' / '+Path(r['coarse']).parent.name,pc(r['full_change']),pc(r['shadow_change'])) for r in a['reference_checks']])
    rows=[r for r in a['comparisons'] if r['engine']=='sionna' and r['shadow_refined'] and r['shadow_refined']['complex_rms'] is not None]
    worst=max(rows,key=lambda r:r['shadow_refined']['complex_rms'])
    groups=table(['Group','Ray shadow RMS','Optional shadow RMS','Reference mesh change','Optional shadow |phase| median / p95'],[(r['name'],pc(r['shadow_ray']['complex_rms']),pc(r['shadow_refined']['complex_rms']),pc(r['reference_shadow_change']['complex_rms']),f'{r["shadow_refined"]["phase_median_abs_deg"]:.3f}° / {r["shadow_refined"]["phase_p95_abs_deg"]:.3f}°') for r in rows])
    local=read(work/'local_candidates.json');localtable=table(['Unfitted diagnostic','Shadow RMS','Median |phase|','95th percentile |phase|'],[(r['candidate'],pc(r['complex_rms']),f'{r["phase_median_abs_deg"]:.3f}°',f'{r["phase_p95_abs_deg"]:.3f}°') for r in local['aggregates'] if r['engine']=='sionna'])
    reciprocity=read(work/'reciprocity_validation.json');boundary=read(work/'boundary_validation.json');resolution=read(work/'resolution_validation.json')
    assert reciprocity['status']==boundary['status']=='passed'
    for name in ('analysis.json','local_candidates.json','study_plan.json','fem_memory_fix.json','fem_workspace_memory_fix.json','reciprocity_validation.json','boundary_validation.json','resolution_validation.json'):shutil.copyfile(work/name,out/name)
    page=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Reducing 3D dielectric shadow error</title>
<style>body{{font:17px/1.65 system-ui;background:#08131d;color:#dce8f0;max-width:1200px;margin:35px auto;padding:0 24px}}h1,h2{{color:#fff;line-height:1.2}}h1{{font-size:38px}}h2{{margin-top:40px}}a{{color:#64d6e8}}table{{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:14px;margin:20px 0}}th,td{{padding:9px;border:1px solid #334a5c;text-align:left}}th{{background:#193345}}video{{width:100%;border-radius:12px}}.box{{padding:18px 24px;background:#122b3b;border-left:4px solid #55d4bd}}pre{{white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere;background:#122b3b;padding:16px}}details{{overflow:auto}}</style><body>
<p><a href="../../../../index.html">All reports</a> · <a href="../dielectric3d_diffraction/index.html">Previous 33.08% investigation</a></p>
<h1>Reducing the 3D dielectric shadow-field error</h1>
<div class="box"><p><strong>A coupled full-wave companion substantially reduces the measured discrepancy.</strong> On the primary shadow population, reciprocal ray RMS is {pc(p['shadow_ray']['complex_rms'])} and the separate full-wave field differs from independent FEM by {pc(p['shadow_refined']['complex_rms'])}. On fresh materials and positions: {pc(h['shadow_ray']['complex_rms'])} → {pc(h['shadow_refined']['complex_rms'])}.</p><p>This is an optional solver shared by both workflows, explicitly scoped to an infinite solid dielectric quadrant. It is not a repaired native UTD coefficient or a general correction for arbitrary scenes. Native ray exports remain intact.</p></div>
<h2>Regenerated comparison</h2><video controls preload="metadata" poster="previews/frame_0601.png" src="Sionna_vs_DiffeRT_Dielectric3D_Refined.mp4"></video>
<p>60 seconds · 1600 × 900 · 24 fps · <a href="Sionna_vs_DiffeRT_Dielectric3D_Refined.mp4">MP4</a> · <a href="Dielectric3D_Refined_Comparison.blend">Blender timeline</a> · <a href="video_validation.json">Decode and reopen check</a>. The two panels show each engine's retained ray workflow beside the same labelled optional field. White curves are independent FEM, gray curves are ray fields, and colored curves are the stated mode. The full-wave field has no ray-path decomposition; accepted native paths are displayed only as geometric context.</p>
<h2>What caused the remaining error?</h2><p>The old 33.08% is a norm over 162 weak shadow channels, not a uniform error per path. Its polarization dependence is large: some crossed-dipole groups exceed 50%, while edge-parallel groups are around 15–23%. The shadow population excludes direct LOS and specular reflection geometrically, so correcting visible LOS cannot remove this discrepancy.</p>
<p>Local UTD is an asymptotic face-based model. The solid-wedge Maxwell problem also includes finite-distance vector fields, coupling of electric and magnetic longitudinal components, and internal dielectric transmission/face coupling. Those effects are all retained in the new companion. Their individual contributions are not uniquely separated by this study; no claim is made that one scalar adjustment explains the entire error.</p>
<p>The observable is the <strong>total field in geometric shadow</strong>, not a uniquely isolated Maxwell diffraction coefficient. Transmission through the solid dielectric can contribute there even though the native benchmark disables refraction. Consequently, the old 33.08% cannot be attributed entirely to a coding error in either native diffraction coefficient. The companion supplies the declared solid-volume field under its own physical model.</p>
{localtable}<p>Multiplying diffracted fields by exact transverse free-dipole endpoint factors was tested without fitting. It improves aggregate RMS to 26.33% on the old reference but worsens phase-tail errors. This incomplete vector correction was not promoted. No gain/phase calibration against reference data was used.</p>
<h2>Correction implemented</h2><p><code>dielectric3d_refinement.py</code> solves the coupled Maxwell transmission boundary integral equations at each longitudinal wavenumber. It reconstructs all electric components, integrates propagating and evanescent modes, and adds the analytic free-space point-dipole dyadic. Defaults are 32 panels per wavelength, geometric refinement next to the corner, 12-point panel quadrature, a 3 λ unstretched boundary plus 3 λ absorbing tail, and 8-point Gaussian integration on each of eight spectral intervals (64 modes). The Fourier contour height is 0.5 and cutoff 24, in units where k₀=2π.</p>
<p>The numerical BIE kernel was previously a cross-check; it is now wrapped as a guarded optional product field. Validation uses the distinct finite-element implementation, including fresh material/geometry holdouts. The production code never reads FEM outputs, expected channels or fitted coefficients. There is no completed-channel reciprocity averaging. Linear residuals and checkpoint hashes are checked before results are accepted.</p>
<p>The physical model is an explicitly declared infinite <em>solid</em> quadrant. Native meshes use long faces and local slabs as a comparison proxy. Adding this companion changes the field model in a separate output; it does not silently reinterpret an arbitrary Blender scene as an infinite volume.</p>
<h2>Results and fresh holdouts</h2>{summary}
<p>The primary set contains 3,888 complete vector channels. The 1,008-channel holdout set uses two new permittivities (3.3−0.48i and 7.1−1.1i), source azimuths 43.5° and 71.25°, source radii 1.25 and 1.8 λ, receiver radius 2.3 λ, nonzero source heights, and new receiver heights and boundary stencils. All nine x/y/z dipole combinations are included. The normal-use resolution was amended from 48 to 32 panels for resource cost, before holdout reference results were available; the successful 48-panel concrete control is retained. Acceptance gates were unchanged: every material and aggregate must have optional shadow RMS below 5% and at least a threefold reduction from the reciprocal ray baseline.</p>
<p>Complex RMS is √(Σ|a−a<sub>FEM</sub>|²/Σ|a<sub>FEM</sub>|²), with no complex rescaling. Shadow masks come from the canonical geometry, excluding ±0.02° around transitions; they are identical for both engines. Relative phase is excluded at reference nulls. The new finer reference revises the old 33.08% baseline slightly, so before/after numbers above use the same current reference.</p>
<p><strong>Material averages do not bound every polarization group.</strong> The largest non-null shadow-group discrepancy is {pc(worst['shadow_refined']['complex_rms'])} for <code>{worst['name']}</code>, with reference-refinement change {pc(worst['reference_shadow_change']['complex_rms'])}. The animation deliberately includes weak crossed-polarization channels with larger residuals. All groups are retained below.</p>
<p>That hardest weak-port discrepancy remains unresolved and is substantially larger than its measured FEM refinement change. Further BIE panel, absorbing-tail and spectral-quadrature sweeps on that port are needed before attributing or correcting it. No scalar calibration was applied to hide the residual.</p>
<details><summary>All non-null shadow groups, including weak crossed-polarization cases</summary>{groups}</details>
<h2>Reciprocity and boundary behavior</h2><p>Separately solved forward and reversed source locations give a raw dyadic reciprocity discrepancy of {pc(reciprocity['relative_reciprocity_error'])}, without averaging the completed channels. Forward discrepancy from FEM is {pc(reciprocity['forward_reference_rms'])}; reverse discrepancy from the Lorentz transpose of that independent FEM dyad is {pc(reciprocity['reverse_reference_rms'])}. The FEM reverse field was not separately solved at this new pair. This checks numerical reciprocity within the declared 5% field tolerance; it does not establish exact discrete reciprocity. <a href="reciprocity_validation.json">Full dyads and scope</a>.</p>
<p>Fresh material and position holdouts include {boundary['unique_stencils']} three-point boundary stencils at the transition and ±0.01°, including all Cartesian port combinations. Every value is finite. Across {boundary['nonnull_stencils']} non-null stencils, maximum refined stencil RMS discrepancy from FEM is {pc(boundary['maximum_refined_stencil_rms'])}. The maximum error in the endpoint-to-endpoint span, normalized by FEM stencil RMS, is {pc(boundary['maximum_refined_span_error'])} for the companion and {pc(boundary['maximum_ray_span_error'])} for the reciprocal ray field. These sampled diagnostics distinguish a physical slope from a numerical jump; they are not a proof of continuity or a new fitted boundary rule. <a href="boundary_validation.json">All measured stencils</a>.</p>
<h2>Independent Maxwell accuracy and remaining uncertainty</h2>{reference}
<p>The retained 48-panel concrete control reduces shadow RMS from {pc(resolution['shadow_standard_rms'])} at 32 panels to {pc(resolution['shadow_fine_rms'])} against FEM64. Changing product resolution changes the shadow field by {pc(resolution['shadow_resolution_change'])}, at fixed Fourier order 8. Finer product and reference discretizations can therefore reduce part of the residual, at higher runtime and memory cost. This control covers concrete; it does not establish a 48-panel holdout result. <a href="resolution_validation.json">Resolution control</a>.</p>
<p>Concrete uses FEM64, the second primary material FEM48, and the two holdouts FEM48 with FEM32 comparisons. The primary comparisons change both grid and Fourier order (6 to 8); holdouts change grid at fixed order 8. These are reference-refinement changes, not isolated spatial error bounds. Earlier analytic dielectric-plane dyadics, exact free-space Fourier synthesis, exact PEC point-wedge checks, enlarged-domain controls and independent reversed-source checks remain applicable to these equations. See the previous report and evidence archive for those controls.</p>
<p><strong>Measured BIE/FEM agreement is not a certified pointwise error bound.</strong> Shadow fields are weaker and more sensitive than the full-channel norm. The table therefore reports convergence specifically on the shadow population. Residual differences comparable to reference mesh changes cannot be assigned entirely to the optional solver. More reference refinement or external full-wave software is needed to establish smaller absolute errors, particularly close to nulls.</p>
<p>The finer FEM run exposed unnecessary memory allocations in diagnostic matrix copies and retained assembly arrays. The implementation now reads SuperLU's stored-entry count and releases unused element/COO arrays before factorization. Independent old/new runs produce bit-identical fields. Checkpoint migrations and source snapshots document these arithmetic-preserving changes; interrupted runs resume only their verified completed modes.</p>
<h2>Supported workflow and obstacle protection</h2><p>Every case is validated before any solver or output runs. The supported domain is the exact canonical 160 λ two-face mesh and edge, λ=1 m, single x/y/z electric point dipoles, port radii 1–2.5 λ, exterior angles 5–265°, absolute height and height difference at most 3 λ, 2≤Re ε≤8 and 0.25≤loss≤1.5. These bounds are a tested numerical envelope, not a uniform-accuracy guarantee over every possible combination.</p>
<p>Changed faces, added obstacles (including a one-micron test obstacle), arrays, unsupported orientations/materials, finite thin slabs, higher interaction orders and other configurations are rejected explicitly. General scenes continue to use the existing native Blender workflow. No real obstacle is removed to enable this correction.</p>
<pre>.\\Compare_Dielectric3D_Wedge.ps1

# Reuse only matching, checksummed numerical steps from this campaign:
.\\Compare_Dielectric3D_Wedge.ps1 -CheckpointFolder work/d3shadow/concrete_standard_product_modes</pre>
<p>The companion runs both configured external engines and writes their unchanged CIR exports beside <code>refinement.json</code> and <code>comparison.json</code>. This NumPy/SciPy full-wave solve is substantially more expensive than local ray coefficients and does not preserve either engine’s automatic-differentiation graph. It returns one complex channel at the declared frequency, without ray delays or broadband CIR taps. A changed scene, numerical parameter or solver source invalidates the checkpoint instead of returning stale fields. The existing <a href="../../../../Launch_Reciprocal_Diffraction.ps1">Blender reciprocal workbench</a> and default native workflow remain available; no upstream runtime or Blender extension file is replaced by this mode.</p>
<h2>Verification and reproduction</h2><p><a href="verification.json">Final verification receipt</a> · <a href="native_workflow_health.json">Fresh native Blender check</a> · <a href="reciprocal_blender.json">Fresh reciprocal Blender check</a> · <a href="dielectric3d_refinement_evidence.zip">Source and numerical evidence archive</a> · <a href="analysis.json">Full measured fields</a> · <a href="study_plan.json">Recorded acceptance plan</a> · <a href="../../../../Regenerate_Dielectric3D_Refined_Animation.ps1">Animation regeneration</a></p>
<p>Source scripts are in <code>work/github-update/differt-bridge/scripts</code>: <code>dielectric3d_shadow_cases.py</code>, <code>dielectric3d_refinement.py</code>, <code>run_dielectric3d_holdout_reference.py</code>, <code>analyze_dielectric3d_refinement.py</code>, and the corresponding report/animation builders. Use OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1. The largest FEM runs should be scheduled with limited concurrency on this 16 GB machine. The evidence retains interrupted-run logs and verified cache migrations.</p>
<h2>Scientific scope</h2><p>A coupled vector transmission formulation is consistent with <a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/qjmam/hbm029">Salem and Kamel's dielectric-wedge work</a>; their abstract reports independent frequency-domain verification. The <a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/TAP.2021.3060079">2021 study of heuristic 3D dielectric-wedge UTD coefficients</a> also explicitly treats internal-reflection boundary behavior. These sources motivate the model distinction; this implementation is not a reproduction of their complete algorithms. <a href="https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.sparse.linalg.SuperLU.html">SciPy's SuperLU interface</a> documents the sparse solve and diagnostic properties used here.</p>
<p>Both numerical Maxwell implementations are project code, independent of the ray tracers but not third-party certification. Finite edges, arbitrary 3D solids, multiple wedges, low-loss materials outside the stated range and general Blender geometry remain unvalidated by this companion. Native UTD model limitations remain in the original ray outputs.</p></body></html>'''
    (out/'index.html').write_text(page,encoding='utf-8');print(out/'index.html')

if __name__=='__main__':main(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve())
